WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
Models should be managed as operational assets, not random files that accumulate over time.

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.039
Names and tags help identify what is being used, but they are not a complete inventory.

3
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:22.666
Teams still need documentation that explains which models are required, which are experimental, which are approved for shared use, which are deprecated, and which can be removed safely.

4
00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:26.345
Storage planning matters because model files can be large.

5
00:00:26.345 --> 00:00:35.103
A quick experiment may consume meaningful disk space, and several experiments can fill a workstation or shared server faster than expected.

6
00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:45.258
When storage runs out, model pulls can fail, applications can break, logs may stop writing, and operators may waste time figuring out which files are safe to delete.

7
00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:50.851
Pulling a model for a personal test is different from approving a model for a team workflow.

8
00:00:50.851 --> 00:01:02.184
Shared use should include basic review: why the model is needed, what hardware it requires, what license or usage expectations apply, who owns it, and how it will be updated or retired.

9
00:01:02.184 --> 00:01:06.011
That review does not need to be heavy, but it should be visible.

10
00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:10.058
Good inventory discipline prevents confusion across machines.

11
00:01:10.058 --> 00:01:18.742
Operators should know where models are stored, how much space they consume, which hosts need which models, and what cleanup routine exists.

12
00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:25.144
Storage planning turns local AI from a collection of one-off downloads into a manageable environment.
